//! member: true
//!
//! std.range — operations over the language's `a..b`. Stability tier:
//! core.
//!
//! **Three of the contract's four functions are BLOCKED, and the reason
//! is one missing thing: a range value has no bounds accessor.** At
//! these pins a `range[int]` can be constructed (`2..7`), passed to a
//! function, and iterated by `for` — and nothing else. `r.start`,
//! `r.end`, `r.len`, `r.lo`, `r.hi`, `r.first`, `r.last` all answer
//! "a range has no member" in the interpreter, and the type `range` does
//! not resolve at all in the compiler (E0301). Filed as F-0030.
//!
//! What that costs, precisely:
//!
//! - `contains(r, x) -> bool` — writable only by iterating, which is
//!   O(len) where the operation is O(1), and a *hang* rather than an
//!   answer on `0..9223372036854775807`. std does not ship a function
//!   that is right on small inputs and fatal on legal ones (sc03's
//!   refusal-over-approximation rule, §9).
//! - `len(r) -> int` — same, and worse: counting to 2^63 is not an
//!   implementation of `len`.
//! - `clamp_to(r, x) -> int` — needs both bounds as values; there is no
//!   spelling at all.
//!
//! `is_empty(r)` survives because emptiness is decidable in O(1) from
//! iteration alone: a range is empty exactly when its iterator yields
//! nothing, and `for` plus an early `return` asks that question without
//! consuming the range. It is the only function here, and it is the
//! demonstration that the other three are blocked by an accessor rather
//! than by iteration.
//!
//! The reviewed signatures, for the sprint that lands them:
//! `contains(r: range[int], x: int) -> bool`,
//! `len(r: range[int]) -> int`,
//! `clamp_to(r: range[int], x: int) -> int`. `rev()` and `step_by()`
//! remain BLOCKED on the iterator combinator surface (F-0008's
//! successor); note that the combinator that truncates a sequence is
//! named `limit(n)`, never `take(n)` — `take` is the ownership mode that
//! CONSUMES its operand, and a combinator that does not consume would be
//! a false friend at the centre of the ownership story (wolf-lang#16,
//! recorded in API-CONVENTIONS §10).
//!
//! Runs on the interpreter only: the compiler does not resolve the type
//! `range` at either rung, so every test here is a lupin lane and the
//! ledger says so. The single function's example is prose rather than a
//! fenced block for exactly that reason — a fenced example must reach
//! `exit(0)` or an honest `unsupported`, and a static rejection is a doc
//! bug by §4.

/// True when `r` yields no values — `5..5` and `7..3` are empty, `2..7`
/// is not. O(1): the loop body runs at most once. Total: no error row,
/// no traps.
///
/// Example (prose; see the module header for why it is not a fenced
/// block): `range.is_empty(5..5)` is true and `range.is_empty(2..7)` is
/// false.
pub fn is_empty(r: range[int]) -> bool {
    for _ in r {
        return false
    }
    true
}
